      "id": 1,
      "text": "Johnsonville earned its name from early settler William Johnson, who arrived in 1816 and built the first mills along the Hoosic River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnsonville%2C_New_York",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1830 arrival of the railroad turned Johnsonville into a bustling rail‑town, linking its axe and bobbin factories to markets across New England.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        4,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/johnsonville_new_york",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1891 Johnsonville Iron Bridge, one of Rensselaer County’s few surviving iron structures, still arches over the Hoosic River today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/johnsonville_new_york",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Johnsonville once housed the world’s largest axe factory, a testament to its 19th‑century industrial boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://alchetron.com/Johnsonville%2C-New-York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Hoosic River, a Hudson tributary, carved Johnsonville’s valley and powered its early mills and lumber shops.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
